{"timestamp": "2026-07-22T01:14:07.058196", "benchmark": "gaia2", "group": "raw_patch", "phase": "test", "task_id": "gaia2_scenario_universe_21_mq2acb", "task_desc": "What is the age of the person who attended the most \"dinner with friends\" events (excluding myself) in the months of August, September and October 2024? If there is a tie, choose the one who sent me an email most recently.", "augmented_prompt": "", "response": "I have the August records and some September pages. I’m paging further to capture the rest of September and October so the count is complete before I resolve the tie-break.\nThe remaining work is to close out the October page and then reduce the attendee list to a count. After that I’ll use contacts for ages and email recency only for any tie.\nI’ve got enough of the event timeline to know the remaining work is purely identification: count the dinner attendees, then resolve any tie using email history, then look up the person’s age. I’m checking the participant list against contacts next so I can avoid guessing names that only differ by accents or spelling.\nI’ve isolated the event family. The last step on the calendar side is to enumerate every exact `Dinner with Friends` hit in the target months so I can count attendees consistently and not mix in dinner dates or family dinners.\nI’m switching to the social-tagged event stream. That gives the full set of dinner-adjacent entries in one place, which is the cleanest way to count without missing a month boundary.\nI need the full event list for each target month, not the paged slices we’ve been seeing. I’m pulling August, September, and October separately with a larger window so I can count the exact dinner events directly.\nI have enough evidence to suspect a small tied set, but I still need the complete matching dinner list before deciding. I’m querying the exact event title across the whole date window with date bounds so I can count only the relevant events.\nThe title search hit the output ceiling, so I’m paging month-by-month instead of relying on a single response. That lets me count the dinner events exactly without assuming the truncated output was complete.\nI still need the tail of each month’s event list to make the count exact. I’m paging deeper now and filtering only the `Dinner with Friends` entries from those responses.\nThe monthly responses are dense enough that paging by offset is awkward. I’m narrowing to week-sized windows now; that keeps each result readable and makes the attendee counts auditable.\nI found another exact dinner entry on Aug 19 and verified its full attendees. I’m going day-level for the remaining ranges where the broader results were clipped, then I’ll resolve the winner through contacts and email timestamps.\nI’m switching to the calendar’s text search without date filters, then I’ll read the matching event records that fall inside August through October. That should be cleaner than paging around clipped month outputs.\nThe direct title search confirms the exact title and the first several matching records, but its result is still capped. I’m querying only evening windows now, which isolates dinner events and avoids unrelated daytime calendar entries.\nI’ve got the August dinners cleanly: Aug 5, 8, 11, 15, 18, 19, and 26. I’m doing the same evening-window pass for the rest of September and all of October.\nSeptember and October have fewer exact “Dinner with Friends” hits than August in the evening windows, but some outputs are still clipped before later events. I’m checking the Social tag and narrower windows to avoid missing any exact-title event.\nThe exact-title search suggests the recurring “Dinner with Friends” set is concentrated in August, but I’m verifying via narrower searches before using the counts. After that I only need the winning contact’s age, unless the count is tied.", "expected": [{"class_name": "OracleEvent", "event_type": "AGENT", "event_time": 1728975601.0, "event_id": "OracleEvent-AGENT-eef8f0e