onversation counts and calendar overlap only among those names.\nI’ve got the Portland candidates from contacts. Next I’m checking calendar history for their names directly so I only keep people who actually have a one-on-one event in the date window, then I’ll rank those by conversation count.\nI’ve narrowed the calendar side to the Portland contacts that actually have a one-on-one event with you. I’m counting their Messages conversations now, because that decides the final name.\nThe conversation-count call wants integers, so I’m rerunning it with the API’s expected types. I’m also checking the tail of the contacts list one more time to make sure I didn’t miss a Portland resident further down.\nI have the full tail of the contact list and the Portland names are now clear. The remaining work is to measure which of those names actually have Messages conversations, then pick the highest count among the ones with a qualifying one-on-one calendar event.I want to rule out early-list Portland contacts before I rank anything. There may be another Portland resident hidden in the first pages, so I’m checking those pages directly instead of assuming the later ones are the whole set.\nI now have the full set of Portland residents from contacts: Kaitlyn, Kaia, Harold, Astrid, Jasper, and Kaidën. I’m resolving their Messages counts next and then I’ll pick the top one among those with a qualifying one-on-one calendar event.\nThe messaging API caps each page at 5 conversations, so I’m paging those candidate histories. I only need counts, not message content, and I’ll stop once a page comes back short.\nThe messaging calls were empty because those APIs key off phone numbers, not contact IDs. I’m pulling the candidate contact records now so I can count conversations against the right identifiers.\nI’ve got the correct phone numbers now. Next I’m using the calendar range directly and matching only events that have exactly one other attendee besides you, so the result is grounded in 